  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CDT, skies are sunny and temperatures range from around 80 \ndegrees along the Minnesota/South Dakota border to the low 90s out \nacross and west of the Missouri River valley. Winds have a southeast \ncomponent to them, 10 to 25 mph with occasional gusts up to 30-35 \nmph. Heat index values are in the low to mid 90s across central and \nnorth central South Dakota.\n\nAn omega-block pattern (trough/ridge/trof) is in place over Canada \nand the United States. This pattern will persist through the weekend \nbefore the pattern flattens out for the middle of the week. It's not \nreal clear how the pattern will look by the end of next week, as \nensembles clusters analysis appears to imply a rex-block may be \nsetting up along/just off of the west coast, with this CWA possibly \nin west or northwest flow coming off that block pattern.\n\nFor the next couple of days, short-range ensembles/hi-res CAMS, \nsupplemented by GSM output, suggest a potentially active weather \npattern of thunderstorms over or moving into this CWA at times. SPC \nhas the CWA in a marginal risk for severe thunderstorms today, \nSunday and Monday.\n\nThere could be a couple of storms to monitor later this \nafternoon/early this evening (mainly west river), as preliminary \nstorms are expected to develop on/near the Black Hills and start \nheading west/northwest toward Stanley/Dewey/Corson counties. Daytime \nheating is beginning to destabilize the BL and the shortwave energy \nover Wyoming is making its way over toward this region. Meso-\nanalysis shows instability working toward ~2000-3500J/kg CAPE and \ndeep layer shear of ~20-35 knots. Still thinking mainly multi-\ncellular storms with wind/hail the main threats with any late \nafternoon/early evening convection. At this point, any convection \nreaching the far western/northwestern zones is not expected to last \nlong or develop into an organized system. Additional convection may \ndevelop later tonight on a low level jet, currently progged to set \nup across northeast South Dakota, eastern North Dakota and the \nnorthern half of Minnesota. Wherever the best low to mid-level WAA \nand moisture advection happens whenever the low level jet sets up is \nwhere/when convection is expected to ramp up (sometime generally \nbetween 07Z and 13Z and somewhere inside a box formed between \nJamestown ND, Wadena MN, Willmar MN and Redfield SD). So, lots of \nuncertainty surrounding tonight's/Sunday morning's convective \npotential. Mid-level shortwave energy moving up through the \nintermountain west should add some synoptic-scale lift for storm \ndevelopment overnight as well.\n\nMonday's weather potential could be occurring at a non-traditional \ntimeframe, if the shortwave energy being advertised to lift out onto \nthe northern plains does so in the morning. Could be facing a line \nof storms, supported by a much stronger (forced) dynamic set-up, \nentering the western forecast zones sometime between 7 AM CDT and 11 \nAM CDT Monday morning. Timing is the biggest question right now.\n\nUnderneath this upper level ridge, the above normal warmth \ncontinues. When the pattern changes next week, the first of a series \nof cold fronts will pass through the region Monday/Monday night. \nThat will signal an airmass change. Slightly cooler but notably \ndrier air will be over the region on Tuesday. Breezy to windy \nnorthwest winds of 15 to 30 mph are expected on Tuesday. Combined \nwith already dry/dormant fine fuels and low relative humidity, a \nportion of the CWA could end up in a fire weather headline on \nTuesday.",
